From d7900e6cd87394021dfa46946985c3d5989c098d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:25:05 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] fixup! Aurora: the HD look's shading leaves the effects drawn
 over the scene alone

Effects that write depth are effects too. Two kinds in Dragon Roost Cavern
weren't, so the sun pass shaded them as scenery:

- The lava geysers: blended, alpha-tested models that write depth, their
  colour from the TEV's registers alone (no texture or rasterized colour).
  In the room's shade they went blue (the shade's ambient over the sunlight).
- The dust a shutting door raises: each particle drawn alpha-tested into the
  depth alone, then in colour over it. The floor under it was shaded and
  fogged at the dust's depth, so the dust showed as dark, sharp-edged holes
  in the room's haze.

Now a blended draw that writes depth is an effect when it is one of the
game's particles (the host's tag) or a glow model (its colour from neither a
texture nor the rasterized colour). A particle's colour draw over its own
depth draw is drawn at its own depth into the effects' mask, as is an effect
that writes depth. Such a pass's effects' mask tests against a copy of the
sea mask's depth, which leaves those effects out: the others first, pushed
back as before, then those at their own depth (a geyser's sparks on its
surface, pushed back against the geyser's depth, kept its shade).

Where such an effect covers a pixel, the occlusion, the sun shadows and the
cloud shadows shade from the sea mask's depth (the scenery under it, which
Wind Waker HD lights before it draws its effects): a small pass makes that
depth only in frames with such effects. Other frames are unchanged (frame
dumps from five save states match the build before within noise).
